{
  "manufacturer": "Endress+Hauser",
  "manufacturer_slug": "endress-hauser",
  "model": "Proline Promag 400 electromagnetic flowmeter (Modbus RS485)",
  "model_slug": "promag-400-modbus",
  "short_model": "Promag 400",
  "aliases": [
    "Promag D 400",
    "Promag L 400",
    "Promag W 400",
    "Proline 400 Modbus RS485"
  ],
  "equipment_type": "Sensor (electromagnetic flowmeter)",
  "applies_to": "Proline Promag 400 transmitters with the Modbus RS485 output, as described in GP01045D/06/EN/02.21.",
  "summary": "The Promag 400 is Endress+Hauser's electromagnetic flowmeter family (D, L and W sensors). Its Modbus RS485 interface exposes every operating-menu parameter at a fixed register. This map lists the values an integrator needs: volume and mass flow, conductivity, three totalizers with their controls, units, diagnostics and the Modbus settings.",
  "transport": [
    "RTU",
    "ASCII"
  ],
  "defaults": {
    "unit_id": 247,
    "baud": 19200,
    "parity": "Even",
    "data_bits": null,
    "stop_bits": null,
    "tcp_port": null
  },
  "addressing": {
    "convention": "1-based",
    "note": "Register numbers are printed in the Modbus Application Protocol form, which the manual equates with Modicon 3XXXX/4XXXX numbers (mass flow 2007 = 32007), and the scan-list parameter says register addresses are 1-based. So subtract 1 for the wire address: volume flow 3874 is requested at 3873."
  },
  "default_word_order": "CDAB",
  "firmware": "Valid from device firmware 02.01.zz",
  "source": {
    "title": "Description of Device Parameters Proline Promag 400 Modbus RS485",
    "doc_number": "GP01045D/06/EN/02.21",
    "revision_date": "2022-02-01",
    "url": "https://bdih-download.endress.com/files/DLA/005056A500261EEC9FD771039688391B/GP01045DEN_0221-00.pdf",
    "pages": "pp. 113-118 (Modbus settings, byte order), p. 174 (address model), pp. 181-194 (register tables)"
  },
  "notes": [
    "This is an integrator subset of a parameter manual that gives a register for every menu item; display, sensor-adjustment, I/O and web-server parameters are left out.",
    "Float word order follows the Byte order parameter (register 4915). At the factory setting 1-0-3-2 the bytes go out as byte 1, byte 0, byte 3, byte 2, with byte 3 holding the sign and exponent, which is CDAB. If you change Byte order, the word order of every float changes with it.",
    "Integer parameters are 2 bytes; the manual does not say signed or unsigned, so they are listed as UINT16 (all values shown are positive codes).",
    "Every value can be read with function 03 or 04 and written with 06 or 16, so it is listed here as a holding register.",
    "The parity picklist codes for the no-parity options disagree between the parameter description (2 = None/1 stop bit) and the register table (2 = None/2 stop bits), so only 0 = Even and 1 = Odd are given.",
    "The diagnostic registers return an integer only; the manual does not list the coding, so read the meaning from the device display or operating manual.",
    "String parameters (device tag, serial number, firmware version, order code) and the scan list (5001-5016, data read back at 5051-5081) are left out because their packing depends on the byte-order setting.",
    "Writes to non-volatile parameters go to EEPROM, which the manual limits to about one million writes; do not write setpoints cyclically.",
    "The data-bit and stop-bit count for the Even/Odd parity settings is not stated in this manual, so those defaults are left blank."
  ],
